/// Log odds of a probability.
///
/// The natural logarithm of the odds `p / (1 - p)`, the inverse of `inv_logit`: 0 for a
/// probability of one half, positive above it and negative below it. `logit(0)` is `-Inf` and
/// `logit(1)` is `Inf`, with the warnings that `log(0)` and a division by zero give (W0204); a
/// probability outside 0 to 1 has a negative odds ratio and a logarithm that is `NA` (W0205).
/// Given a column, it applies to every row in one pass.
///
/// p: A probability, or a column of probabilities.
/// returns: The log odds, a Float; a column when `p` is one.
/// details: Computed as `log(p / (1 - p))`, as R's `qlogis` does, so near 1 it loses the digits that `1 - p` loses; for a probability that small a difference from one, work with the odds instead.
/// reference: Johnson, N. L., Kotz, S. and Balakrishnan, N. (1995) Continuous Univariate Distributions, volume 2, 2nd edition, chapter 23 (the logistic distribution).
/// reference: R Core Team, the `qlogis` function of the stats package.
/// see also: inv_logit, log, exp
/// > logit(0.5)
/// 0
/// > logit(0.9)
/// 2.19722
/// > logit(0.1)
/// -2.19722
/// > logit(0)
/// -Inf
/// warning: log of zero gave -Inf (line 4)
///   = help: `log1p(x)` is log(1 + x) and is finite at zero; or keep the rows with `where(x > 0)`
/// > logit(1)
/// Inf
/// warning: division by zero gave Inf (line 5)
///   = help: test the divisor first, as in `b != 0 && a / b > 1`; on a frame, `where(b != 0)`
fn logit(p: Float) -> Float {
    log(p / (1 - p))
}
